# Cloudflare Workers assets ignore
# GitHub still tracks everything normally — this only affects what CF uploads.

# Raw shelf photos (large HEIC files, multi-GB total)
Floors/

# JSON source (already baked into index.html)
biomed-shelf-ranges.json

# Build tooling
.build_locator.py
*.py

# Docs. Listed by wildcard rather than by name: the assets directory is the repo root, so a
# new .md is published the moment it is written, and SEARCH-REPORT.md in particular is a
# working document about another team's software. It should reach people because it was
# handed to them, not because it was left reachable.
Instructions.txt
*.md

# Test corpus + harness (saved SRU responses; the app never reads these at runtime)
fixtures/
Tools/

# Git internals (CF was choking on the 991 MB pack file)
.git/

# Local tooling state. "assets.directory" is the repo root, so anything not listed here is
# served publicly — .wrangler/cache/wrangler-account.json carries the account id and the
# account's email address, and both of these once shipped by accident. Not credentials
# (the OAuth token lives outside the repo), but not for publication either.
.wrangler/
.claude/
.gitattributes
.assetsignore
.gitignore

# Source, not site
ios/

# Misc downloads that ended up in the repo
*.exe
*.zip
*.msix
*.xz
*.tar
# Scans and other paperwork that landed here. This was `*.pdf`, which is the right instinct and
# the wrong mechanism now that one PDF is meant to ship: gitignore negation would work, but a
# `!rule` that Cloudflare silently ignored would look identical to one that worked, and the file
# would just not be there. So the excluded files are named, and Tools/assets.test.js asserts the
# published set exactly — a new PDF dropped in this directory fails that test rather than
# quietly appearing on the web.
BookScanCenter.pdf
src/

# The deployment's own configuration. Nothing secret in it, but it is how the site is built
# rather than part of the site, and it was being served at /wrangler.jsonc.
wrangler.jsonc
